The default scenario executes modules on the inventory target against an
installed foreground fixture program. It updates existing objects, checks
idempotence, reads public state and predicts a change without writing. Its
process-fault helper runs the public module in another process, injects lost
replies and nonzero exits, and verifies failed readback remains unknown without
redispatch.

## Requirements

The caller supplies an authorized target with Python 3.9+, GNU `timeout`,
`/usr/bin/env`, the installed collection and `foundata.utils`. The standalone
process harness also requires a supported ansible-core on that target. Install
the fixture program from `tests/integration/targets/setup_fixture_cli/files/`
and seed a new mode-0700 directory with `seed.py`. Use one exclusively owned
directory per scenario run. The locator and its numeric user must describe that
target.

Put the connection object in a mode-0600 JSON file on the controller and set
`MOLECULE_PRODUCT_CREDENTIALS` to its absolute path. Its members are
`credential`, `deadline` and `exec`, as the modules document. Credentials are
never command-line arguments. Replace the inventory's local connection with the
authorized host connection when testing remotely. Installation and target
provisioning remain caller prerequisites.

## Run the scenario

```sh
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule -- test
```

The sequence is syntax, cleanup, converge, idempotence, verify, side effect and
cleanup. Cleanup restores the ordinary baseline values; it cannot delete objects
because the fixture exposes no deletion command. The caller removes the owned
state directory and credentials after the run.

## Adapt the scenario

Replace the fixture commands and seeded state from product evidence. Keep lost
replies, immediate status 124, observed effects and unavailable readback
distinct. A native status never establishes deadline expiry. Qualify every
admitted locator's process cleanup and native concurrency guarantees.

Shared playbooks dispatch task files by phase. Product tasks are under
`default/tasks/`; `shared/files/loss.py` is the CLI invariant process harness. A
product supplies the fault-control and write-receipt paths, cases, expected
results and private values through stdin. Each case must dispatch exactly one
write. Extend the invariant in the skeleton when another operation needs a
different receipt model.
